# January – March. (date, calendar title, display name, born, died, patron of, life)
# Written for Catholic My Pride. Short summaries; the admin can expand or correct any of them.
DATA = [
('01-01', 'Blessed Virgin Mary, the Mother of God', 'Mary, the Holy Mother of God', '', '', ['mothers', 'all Christians'],
 'On the first day of the year the Church honours Mary under her greatest title: Mother of God. The Council of Ephesus (431) proclaimed her *Theotokos*, "God-bearer", because the child she bore is truly God made man. Her quiet "yes" to the angel opened the way for our salvation, and she remains the model of faith for every disciple.\n\nWe begin each year under her care, asking her to bring us closer to her Son, the Prince of Peace.'),
('01-02', 'Saints Basil the Great and Gregory Nazianzen, bishops and doctors', 'Saints Basil the Great and Gregory Nazianzen', 'c. 329–330', '379 and c. 390', ['monks', 'theologians'],
 'Basil and Gregory were close friends from their student days in Athens. Basil became Bishop of Caesarea in Cappadocia, wrote a rule that shaped Eastern monastic life and built a great centre of care for the poor and sick. Gregory, a gifted preacher and poet, served briefly as Bishop of Constantinople.\n\nTogether they defended the faith in the divinity of Christ and of the Holy Spirit against the Arian heresy, and both are honoured as Doctors of the Church.'),
('01-07', 'Saint Raymond of Penyafort, priest', 'Saint Raymond of Penyafort', 'c. 1175, near Barcelona, Spain', '1275, Barcelona', ['canon lawyers'],
 'Raymond was a brilliant teacher of law in Barcelona before joining the Dominicans. At the request of Pope Gregory IX he gathered the Church\'s laws into one great collection that was used for centuries. Elected Master General of the Dominicans, he resigned after two years to return to pastoral work, preaching and hearing confessions.\n\nHe encouraged the study of Arabic and Hebrew so that the Gospel could be shared with all peoples, and lived to almost a hundred.'),
('01-13', 'Saint Hilary of Poitiers, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Hilary of Poitiers', 'c. 315, Poitiers, Gaul', 'c. 368', ['against snakebites'],
 'Hilary was a married man who came to faith through reading the Scriptures and was later chosen Bishop of Poitiers in France. He courageously defended the divinity of Christ against the Arian emperors and was exiled for it to Asia Minor, where he wrote his great work *On the Trinity*.\n\nHis clear teaching earned him the title "Athanasius of the West", and he is honoured as a Doctor of the Church.'),
('01-17', 'Saint Anthony of Egypt, abbot', 'Saint Anthony of Egypt', 'c. 251, Egypt', '356, Egypt', ['monks', 'farmers', 'gravediggers'],
 'Hearing the Gospel words "Go, sell what you have and give to the poor," the young Anthony did exactly that and went into the Egyptian desert to live for God alone. For decades he prayed, fasted and battled temptation. Many disciples gathered around him, and he became known as the father of monasticism.\n\nSaint Athanasius wrote his life, which inspired countless men and women to give themselves wholly to God.'),
('01-20', 'Saint Fabian, pope and martyr', 'Saint Fabian', '', '250, Rome', [],
 'Fabian was a layman visiting Rome when, according to tradition, a dove settled on his head during the papal election, and the people acclaimed him pope. He governed the Church wisely for fourteen years, organising Rome into districts for the care of the poor.\n\nHe was among the first to die in the persecution of Emperor Decius in 250.'),
('01-20', 'Saint Sebastian, martyr', 'Saint Sebastian', '', 'c. 288, Rome', ['soldiers', 'athletes'],
 'Sebastian was a Christian officer in the Roman army who used his position to encourage believers in prison. When his faith was discovered he was shot with arrows and left for dead. Nursed back to health, he boldly confronted the emperor again and was beaten to death.\n\nHe has long been honoured as a model of courage and loyalty to Christ.'),
('01-20', 'Blessed Cyprian Michael Iwene Tansi, priest', 'Blessed Cyprian Michael Iwene Tansi', '1903, Aguleri, Nigeria', '20 January 1964, Leicester, England', ['Nigeria', 'priests'],
 'Iwene Tansi was born to a traditional family in Aguleri, in today\'s Anambra State, and was baptised Michael as a boy. After working as a teacher he was ordained a priest for the Archdiocese of Onitsha in 1937, one of the first Nigerian priests. For thirteen years he served tirelessly in parishes, known for his prayer, his care for families and his strict but loving guidance.\n\nIn 1950 he became a Trappist monk at Mount Saint Bernard Abbey in England, taking the name Cyprian, hoping one day to bring monastic life to Africa. He died there in 1964. Pope John Paul II beatified him in Onitsha in 1998, the first Nigerian to be beatified.'),
('01-21', 'Saint Agnes, virgin and martyr', 'Saint Agnes', 'c. 291, Rome', 'c. 304, Rome', ['young girls', 'chastity'],
 'Agnes was only about twelve or thirteen when she refused marriage, saying she had already given herself to Christ. Denounced as a Christian, she faced threats and torture with astonishing calm and was put to death in Rome.\n\nHer name is remembered in the Roman Canon of the Mass, and lambs blessed on her feast provide wool for the pallium worn by archbishops.'),
('01-22', 'Saint Vincent, deacon and martyr', 'Saint Vincent of Saragossa', '', '304, Valencia, Spain', ['winemakers'],
 'Vincent served as deacon to Bishop Valerius of Saragossa in Spain, preaching on the bishop\'s behalf. During the persecution of Diocletian he was arrested and cruelly tortured, yet remained joyful and firm in the faith until he died.\n\nHe is the most celebrated martyr of Spain.'),
('01-24', 'Saint Francis de Sales, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Francis de Sales', '1567, Savoy', '1622, Lyon, France', ['writers', 'journalists', 'the deaf'],
 'Francis gave up a promising legal career to become a priest. Sent to the Chablais region, he won thousands back to the Catholic faith through patience and gentle preaching, even slipping leaflets under doors. As Bishop of Geneva he wrote *Introduction to the Devout Life*, teaching that holiness is possible for everyone in every walk of life.\n\nWith Saint Jane Frances de Chantal he founded the Visitation Sisters. He is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('01-25', 'The Conversion of Saint Paul, apostle', 'The Conversion of Saint Paul', '', '', ['missionaries', 'evangelists'],
 'Saul of Tarsus was a zealous persecutor of the first Christians until, on the road to Damascus, the risen Jesus appeared to him and asked, "Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?" Blinded, then healed and baptised, he became Paul, the great Apostle to the Gentiles.\n\nHis conversion shows that no one is beyond the reach of God\'s grace.'),
('01-26', 'Saints Timothy and Titus, bishops', 'Saints Timothy and Titus', '', '1st century', [],
 'Timothy and Titus were trusted companions and co-workers of Saint Paul. Timothy, whose mother and grandmother taught him the faith, became leader of the Church in Ephesus; Titus was entrusted with the Church in Crete. Paul wrote letters to both, full of guidance on caring for God\'s people.\n\nThey remind us that the Gospel is handed on from generation to generation.'),
('01-27', 'Saint Angela Merici, virgin', 'Saint Angela Merici', '1474, Desenzano, Italy', '1540, Brescia, Italy', ['teachers', 'the sick'],
 'Angela was convinced that strong Christian families needed well-formed women. In Brescia she gathered a group of young women who lived consecrated lives in their own homes while teaching girls the faith.\n\nIn 1535 she founded the Company of Saint Ursula, the Ursulines, the first order of women dedicated to education.'),
('01-28', 'Saint Thomas Aquinas, priest and doctor', 'Saint Thomas Aquinas', '1225, Roccasecca, Italy', '1274, Fossanova, Italy', ['students', 'teachers', 'Catholic schools'],
 'Thomas joined the Dominicans against his family\'s wishes. A quiet student nicknamed "the dumb ox", he became one of the greatest minds in history. His *Summa Theologiae* shows how faith and reason work together. He also wrote beautiful hymns for the Eucharist, such as *Pange Lingua*.\n\nNear the end of his life he said that all he had written seemed like straw compared with what God had shown him. He is the "Angelic Doctor".'),
('01-31', 'Saint John Bosco, priest', 'Saint John Bosco', '1815, near Turin, Italy', '1888, Turin', ['young people', 'students', 'editors'],
 'Don Bosco gave his life to the poor and abandoned boys of industrial Turin. He offered them shelter, schooling, trades and, above all, the love of Christ, using a method of reason, religion and kindness. To continue the work he founded the Salesians and, with Saint Mary Mazzarello, the Salesian Sisters.\n\nToday his family serves young people in more than 130 countries, including Nigeria.'),
('02-03', 'Saint Ansgar, bishop', 'Saint Ansgar', '801, Picardy, France', '865, Bremen, Germany', ['Scandinavia'],
 'Ansgar was a Benedictine monk who answered the call to bring the Gospel to Denmark and Sweden. As Archbishop of Hamburg he persevered through many setbacks, including the burning of his city.\n\nHe is called the Apostle of the North.'),
('02-03', 'Saint Blase, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Blase', '', 'c. 316, Sebaste, Armenia', ['throat illnesses', 'doctors'],
 'Blase was a bishop in Sebaste, Armenia, martyred in the early fourth century. Tradition tells that he saved a boy who was choking on a fish bone.\n\nOn his feast many parishes bless throats with two crossed candles, praying for protection from illness.'),
('02-05', 'Saint Agatha, virgin and martyr', 'Saint Agatha', 'Sicily', 'c. 251, Catania, Sicily', ['nurses', 'breast cancer patients'],
 'Agatha was a young Christian woman of Sicily who consecrated her life to God. When she refused the advances of a Roman official, he had her tortured and imprisoned, and she died a martyr.\n\nShe is named in the Roman Canon of the Mass.'),
('02-06', 'Saints Paul Miki and companions, martyrs', 'Saints Paul Miki and Companions', 'c. 1562, Japan', '1597, Nagasaki, Japan', ['Japan'],
 'Paul Miki was a Japanese Jesuit and a powerful preacher. In 1597, during a persecution, he and twenty-five companions, priests, brothers, catechists and boys among them, were marched to Nagasaki and crucified.\n\nFrom his cross Paul forgave his persecutors. Japanese Christians kept the faith in secret for over two centuries after them.'),
('02-08', 'Saint Jerome Emiliani, priest', 'Saint Jerome Emiliani', '1486, Venice', '1537, Somasca, Italy', ['orphans', 'abandoned children'],
 'Jerome was a Venetian soldier who, after being captured in war, gave his life to God. Moved by the suffering caused by famine and plague, he cared for orphans, the homeless and the sick, and founded the Somascan Fathers.\n\nHe died caring for plague victims.'),
('02-08', 'Saint Josephine Bakhita, virgin', 'Saint Josephine Bakhita', 'c. 1869, Darfur, Sudan', '1947, Schio, Italy', ['Sudan', 'victims of human trafficking'],
 'Kidnapped as a child in Sudan and sold into slavery, Bakhita suffered terrible cruelty. Brought to Italy, she came to know Christ through the Canossian Sisters, was baptised and won her freedom. She joined the sisters and served humbly as cook and doorkeeper, known for her gentleness.\n\nShe said she would even thank her kidnappers, because without them she would not have known God. She is a patron of those who suffer trafficking today.'),
('02-10', 'Saint Scholastica, virgin', 'Saint Scholastica', 'c. 480, Nursia, Italy', 'c. 543, Monte Cassino, Italy', ['nuns'],
 'Scholastica was the twin sister of Saint Benedict and led a community of nuns near his monastery. The siblings met once a year to talk about God. At their last meeting she prayed for him to stay longer, and a sudden storm kept him there all night.\n\nShe died three days later, and Benedict saw her soul rise to heaven like a dove.'),
('02-11', 'Our Lady of Lourdes', 'Our Lady of Lourdes', '', '', ['the sick'],
 'In 1858 the Virgin Mary appeared eighteen times to Bernadette Soubirous, a poor girl of Lourdes in France. She called people to prayer and penance and named herself "the Immaculate Conception". A spring revealed at the grotto has been linked with many healings.\n\nThis day is also the World Day of the Sick, when the Church prays for all who suffer.'),
('02-14', 'Saints Cyril, monk, and Methodius, bishop', 'Saints Cyril and Methodius', '9th century, Thessalonica', '869 and 885', ['Europe', 'Slavic peoples'],
 'The brothers Cyril and Methodius were Greek missionaries to the Slavic peoples. They created an alphabet for the Slavonic language so that the Scriptures and the liturgy could be shared in the people\'s own tongue.\n\nThey are honoured as Apostles of the Slavs and patrons of Europe.'),
('02-17', 'Seven Holy Founders of the Servite Order', 'The Seven Holy Founders of the Servites', '', '13th century', [],
 'Seven merchants of Florence left their businesses in the 13th century to live a life of prayer and penance together, devoted to the Virgin Mary.\n\nTheir community became the Order of Servants of Mary, the Servites, known especially for devotion to Our Lady of Sorrows.'),
('02-21', 'Saint Peter Damian, bishop and doctor of the Church', 'Saint Peter Damian', '1007, Ravenna, Italy', '1072, Faenza, Italy', [],
 'Orphaned young, Peter Damian became a monk and a great reformer of the Church. He fought corruption among clergy, encouraged prayer and penance, and was made a cardinal.\n\nHis writings on the spiritual life earned him the title Doctor of the Church.'),
('02-22', 'Chair of Saint Peter, apostle', 'The Chair of Saint Peter', '', '', ['the Pope'],
 'Today the Church celebrates the mission Christ gave to Peter: "You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my Church." The "chair" is a sign of the authority to teach and to shepherd.\n\nWe give thanks for the gift of the Pope and pray for him as the successor of Peter.'),
('02-23', 'Saint Polycarp, bishop and martyr', 'Saint Polycarp', 'c. 69', 'c. 155, Smyrna', ['against earaches'],
 'Polycarp was a disciple of Saint John the Apostle and Bishop of Smyrna for many years. Arrested in old age and told to curse Christ, he replied: "Eighty-six years I have served him, and he has done me no wrong. How can I blaspheme my King who saved me?"\n\nHe was burned and stabbed to death, one of the first martyrs whose death was recorded.'),
('02-27', 'Saint Gregory of Narek, abbot and doctor of the Church', 'Saint Gregory of Narek', 'c. 950, Armenia', 'c. 1003, Narek, Armenia', ['Armenia'],
 'Gregory was an Armenian monk, poet and mystic. His *Book of Lamentations*, a series of heartfelt prayers to God from the depths of the soul, is treasured by Armenian Christians.\n\nPope Francis named him a Doctor of the Church in 2015.'),
('03-04', 'Saint Casimir', 'Saint Casimir', '1458, Kraków, Poland', '1484, Grodno', ['Poland', 'Lithuania'],
 'Casimir was a prince of Poland who preferred prayer to the life of court. He lived simply, loved the poor and was deeply devoted to the Blessed Virgin Mary.\n\nHe died of tuberculosis at twenty-five.'),
('03-07', 'Saints Perpetua and Felicity, martyrs', 'Saints Perpetua and Felicity', '', '203, Carthage, North Africa', ['mothers'],
 'Perpetua, a young noblewoman and new mother, and Felicity, a slave who gave birth in prison, were catechumens in Carthage in North Africa. Refusing to deny Christ, they were put to death in the arena with their companions.\n\nPerpetua\'s own prison diary tells their story. They are named in the Roman Canon.'),
('03-08', 'Saint John of God, religious', 'Saint John of God', '1495, Portugal', '1550, Granada, Spain', ['hospitals', 'nurses', 'the sick'],
 'After a restless life as a soldier and shepherd, John was moved by a sermon to give everything to God. He began caring for the sick and poor in Granada, carrying them on his back to his small hospital.\n\nHis followers became the Brothers Hospitallers, who still serve the sick worldwide.'),
('03-09', 'Saint Frances of Rome, religious', 'Saint Frances of Rome', '1384, Rome', '1440, Rome', ['drivers', 'widows'],
 'Frances was a wife and mother in a noble Roman family who cared tirelessly for the poor and sick, especially during plague and war. She said, "A married woman must sometimes leave God at the altar to find him in her household care."\n\nAfter her husband\'s death she joined the community of women she had founded.'),
('03-17', 'Saint Patrick, bishop', 'Saint Patrick', 'c. 385, Britain', 'c. 461, Ireland', ['Ireland', 'engineers'],
 'Kidnapped from Britain as a teenager and enslaved in Ireland, Patrick turned to God in prayer. He escaped, became a priest and returned as a missionary bishop to the people who had enslaved him.\n\nHis preaching brought the faith to all of Ireland. Tradition says he used the shamrock to explain the Holy Trinity.'),
('03-18', 'Saint Cyril of Jerusalem, bishop and doctor', 'Saint Cyril of Jerusalem', 'c. 315, Jerusalem', '386, Jerusalem', [],
 'Cyril was Bishop of Jerusalem and was exiled three times during the Arian controversies. His *Catecheses*, talks given to those preparing for Baptism, are a treasure of early teaching on the sacraments and the Eucharist.\n\nHe is a Doctor of the Church.'),
('03-19', 'Saint Joseph Husband of the Blessed Virgin Mary', 'Saint Joseph, Spouse of the Blessed Virgin Mary', '', '', ['the universal Church', 'fathers', 'workers', 'a happy death'],
 'Joseph, a just man of the house of David, was chosen by God to be husband of Mary and guardian of Jesus. Without speaking a single recorded word in the Gospels, he obeyed God promptly, protecting the Holy Family through danger and exile and teaching Jesus his trade as a carpenter.\n\nHe is patron of the universal Church, of fathers and of workers, and of a happy death.'),
('03-23', 'Saint Turibius of Mogrovejo, bishop', 'Saint Turibius of Mogrovejo', '1538, Spain', '1606, Peru', ['Peru', 'Latin American bishops'],
 'Turibius was a Spanish lawyer, not yet a priest, when he was appointed Archbishop of Lima in Peru. He travelled tens of thousands of kilometres to visit his people, learned local languages, defended the native peoples against abuse and founded the first seminary in the Americas.\n\nHe confirmed Saint Rose of Lima and Saint Martin de Porres.'),
]
